ke/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ead70b80d9256e1211addc404613f2dfe4a19baf822cbd77f429572ffc9a3a6d.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "01ae30657cc075b3bf827e699e81b7553edf9f4a4101aca1cb230f5da63669f8" "basis" 1230 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/d111b1df75cfaa0d86d17c06584c57b19ca4711ae3e6720e68d1704fd72d86ae.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/dd0496914b35f969896c6a54907fbfa5ea6c139c878d8682b933a8757bde7524.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a94f00ef0d1589267e275ebae786a4898c09a90a8bed2c5352a2c73a403dd5c" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81450" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(1,-1),(-2,3),(-1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-3,-2)$\n- $(0,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,1),(-3,-2),(0,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a94f00ef0d1589267e275ebae786a4898c09a90a8bed2c5352a2c73a403dd5c" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "a5c4668c5142ccaa1e834ae87585f527664d5ca4359d1a4186d1b99f9a76dd80" "basis" 1353 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-8394" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,0),(1,5),(2,3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-5,1)$\n- $(0,-2)$\n- $(2,-3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068585/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4a4bd88a37721b77d2c6989ffec506d7feab9df18521003fa8e3fbee91ce7a"} {"question" "q-8394" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(0,0),(-5,1),(-3,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068585/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d4a4bd88a37721b77d2c6989ffec506d7feab9df18521003fa8e3fbee91ce7a"} {"question" "q-8394" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068585/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c1821d28d44cb3de5d913ff6962ac57fabae36b0a04bf946154936bfd5f774e5"} {"question" "q-8394" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Acade